{
  "dom_id": "step-q120535",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222139/assets/8fa5e5b2300c3395d48f5355352cd6ece7477f3bae8e8c185f0f869fa197b0e9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2)+(x-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222139/assets/5a1821ff500c901d9f072fe56101f17e579018d0710543c4a2e371214894725c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
